---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { isHeld } from '../data/publish-queue.js';

const held = isHeld('/ai-crawler-verification/');
---
<BaseLayout
  title="AI Crawler Verification: Reverse DNS Checks for GPTBot, ClaudeBot and More | PromptDNS"
  description="Verify AI crawler visitors with reverse DNS: the dig method for GPTBot, ClaudeBot, PerplexityBot and CCBot, and why spoofed tokens fail it."
  breadcrumb={[["Home", "/"], ["AI Web Infrastructure", "/ai-web-infrastructure/"], ["AI Crawler Verification: Confirm a Crawler Is Genuine with Reverse DNS"]]}
>
  <div class="mx-auto max-w-4xl px-4 py-12">
    <nav class="mb-4 text-sm text-ink-faint" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
      <a href="/" class="hover:text-accent">Home</a> <span aria-hidden="true">/</span>
      <a href="/ai-web-infrastructure/" class="hover:text-accent">AI Web Infrastructure</a> <span aria-hidden="true">/</span>
      <span class="text-ink-dim">AI Crawler Verification: Confirm a Crawler Is Genuine with Reverse DNS</span>
    </nav>
    <h1 class="font-display text-3xl font-bold tracking-tight text-ink sm:text-4xl">AI Crawler Verification: Confirm a Crawler Is Genuine with Reverse DNS</h1>
    <div class="pd-answer" data-vs="answer">
      <p class="text-lg leading-relaxed text-ink-dim">AI crawler verification is the reverse DNS check on the fetching IP address. A genuine GPTBot, ClaudeBot, PerplexityBot, or CCBot fetch resolves to the operator's own address space, while a spoofed token does not. The check matters because user-agent strings are trivial to fake, and security decisions should rest on the IP, not the header.</p>
    </div>

    <section class="not-prose my-8" aria-label="Decision table">
      <div class="glass rounded-2xl p-5 shadow-lg sm:p-7 overflow-x-auto">
        <table class="pdns-table" style="width:100%">
          <thead><tr><th>V</th><th>e</th><th>r</th><th>i</th><th>f</th><th>i</th><th>c</th><th>a</th><th>t</th><th>i</th><th>o</th><th>n</th><th> </th><th>m</th><th>e</th><th>t</th><th>h</th><th>o</th><th>d</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody><tr><td class="font-medium text-ink">G</td></tr><tr><td class="font-medium text-ink">e</td></tr><tr><td class="font-medium text-ink">n</td></tr><tr><td class="font-medium text-ink">u</td></tr><tr><td class="font-medium text-ink">i</td></tr><tr><td class="font-medium text-ink">n</td></tr><tr><td class="font-medium text-ink">e</td></tr><tr><td class="font-medium text-ink"> </td></tr><tr><td class="font-medium text-ink">f</td></tr><tr><td class="font-medium text-ink">e</td></tr><tr><td class="font-medium text-ink">t</td></tr><tr><td class="font-medium text-ink">c</td></tr><tr><td class="font-medium text-ink">h</td></tr><tr><td class="font-medium text-ink"> </td></tr><tr><td class="font-medium text-ink">r</td></tr><tr><td class="font-medium text-ink">e</td></tr><tr><td class="font-medium text-ink">s</td></tr><tr><td class="font-medium text-ink">o</td></tr><tr><td class="font-medium text-ink">l</td></tr><tr><td class="font-medium text-ink">v</td></tr><tr><td class="font-medium text-ink">e</td></tr><tr><td class="font-medium text-ink">s</td></tr><tr><td class="font-medium text-ink"> </td></tr><tr><td class="font-medium text-ink">t</td></tr><tr><td class="font-medium text-ink">o</td></tr></tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
    </section>

    <article class="rich-text mt-10 max-w-none">
      
      <h2>The reverse DNS method</h2>
      <p class="mt-3">Take the fetching IP from your server or WAF logs and run a PTR lookup on it.</p>
      <p class="mt-3">A genuine OpenAI fetch resolves inside the openai.com space, Anthropic inside anthropic.com, Perplexity inside perplexity.ai, and Common Crawl inside commoncrawl.org.</p>
      <p class="mt-3">For defense in depth, forward-confirm the result: resolve the returned hostname back to an IP and check it matches the original visitor. This is FCrDNS, the same standard email verification uses.</p>
      <h2>Where crawlers publish their ranges</h2>
      <p class="mt-3">OpenAI publishes its IP ranges publicly, so you can allow-list or match by range instead of by single lookup.</p>
      <p class="mt-3">Anthropic and Perplexity document verification by reverse DNS on their help pages.</p>
      <p class="mt-3">Common Crawl and Google both resolve cleanly; Google-Extended is the exception, because it is a training control rather than a crawler and never appears in logs.</p>
      <h2>The exception that proves the rule</h2>
      <p class="mt-3">Bytespider reverse DNS is inconsistent, which is why the recommendation for that crawler is edge-level blocking by autonomous system rather than token matching.</p>
      <p class="mt-3">Any crawler whose verification is unreliable should be handled at the WAF, not in robots.txt.</p>

      <aside class="mt-8 rounded-xl border-l-3 border-l-violet/50 bg-violet/5 p-5" aria-label="Practitioner field notes">
        <p class="font-mono text-[10px] uppercase tracking-[0.16em] text-violet mb-2">Field notes</p>
        <p class="text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-dim italic">Verification is the step teams skip and then regret: someone reads a GPTBot spike in the logs, panics, blocks all of OpenAI at the firewall, and takes out their own ChatGPT Search presence in the same move. One reverse DNS check on two sample IPs would have shown which token was real and what it was doing.</p>
      </aside>

      <h2 class="font-display mt-10 text-xl font-semibold text-ink">Frequently asked questions</h2>
      <div class="mt-3 grid gap-3">
        <details class="group rounded-xl border border-line bg-panel/60 px-5 py-4 open:border-accent/40"><summary class="flex cursor-pointer list-none items-center justify-between gap-4 font-semibold text-ink [&::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden">Why is reverse DNS better than the user-agent string?<span class="text-accent transition-transform duration-200 group-open:rotate-45">+</span></summary><p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-dim">User-agent strings are trivial to spoof; a reverse DNS lookup ties the visitor to infrastructure the operator actually controls.</p></details><details class="group rounded-xl border border-line bg-panel/60 px-5 py-4 open:border-accent/40"><summary class="flex cursor-pointer list-none items-center justify-between gap-4 font-semibold text-ink [&::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden">What is FCrDNS?<span class="text-accent transition-transform duration-200 group-open:rotate-45">+</span></summary><p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-dim">Forward-confirmed reverse DNS: the PTR hostname is resolved back to an IP and must match the original visitor. It closes the loop on hostname spoofing.</p></details><details class="group rounded-xl border border-line bg-panel/60 px-5 py-4 open:border-accent/40"><summary class="flex cursor-pointer list-none items-center justify-between gap-4 font-semibold text-ink [&::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden">Do any AI crawlers publish IP ranges?<span class="text-accent transition-transform duration-200 group-open:rotate-45">+</span></summary><p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-dim">Yes, OpenAI publishes its ranges. Others are verified by reverse DNS on the documented domain.</p></details>
      </div>

      <p class="mt-8 text-xs text-ink-faint">
        Related:
        <a href="/ai-crawlers-list/" class="text-accent hover:text-accent-soft">all AI crawlers</a>,
        <a href="/ai-crawler-checker/" class="text-accent hover:text-accent-soft">AI crawler checker</a>,
        <a href="/does-blocking-ai-crawlers-hurt-seo/" class="text-accent hover:text-accent-soft">does blocking AI crawlers hurt SEO</a>.
      </p>
    </article>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
